<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Pollilur (1780)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10 September <a href="/years/1780">1780</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/september-10">On this day: 10 September</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-mysore-war">Second Anglo-Mysore War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Pollilur was fought on 10 September 1780 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-mysore-war">Second Anglo-Mysore War</a>. Forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> surrounded and destroyed a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> detachment under Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-baillie">William Baillie</a>, most of whose men were killed or captured. It was one of the worst defeats suffered by the Company in India, later celebrated in a mural at Tipu's summer palace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-pollilur-1780.jpg" alt="Battle of Pollilur (1780)" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles H. Hubbell (1898-1971) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rocket_warfare.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle, also known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pullalur">Battle of Pullalur</a>, Polilore or Perambakam, was fought at Pollilur near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Conjeevaram</a>, the city of Kanchipuram in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>. Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-baillie">William Baillie</a>'s brigade column of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> was trying to join another Company force under Hector Munro at Conjeevaram, which consisted of two companies of European infantry, two batteries of artillery and five battalions of native infantry from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntur">Guntur</a>. Tipu Sultan kept Baillie from reaching it, while his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> continued the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Baillie's men, suffering desertions and uncoordinated leadership, formed a defensive square on a patch of high ground, with Baillie himself leading a final stand. Cut off from both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Conjeevaram</a> and the larger Company force at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-st-george">Fort St. George</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, they were caught in a double envelopment, encircled and routed. Only 50 European officers and 150 men were taken prisoner after what was described as a general massacre.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> rockets played a part in the fighting. Their iron tubes for the propellant gave them more thrust and a range of up to 2 km, well beyond what the Company had met before. At Pollilur they held up the British vanguard, skimming along the ground and wounding troops; one shattered an ensign's leg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Baillie and many of his officers were taken to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Seringapatam</a>. It was the worst loss the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> suffered on the subcontinent until Chillianwala, and the French officer Benoît de Boigne wrote that there was not in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> an example of a similar defeat. After reinforcements arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eyre-coote">Eyre Coote</a> stabilised the situation and counter-attacked, and a second battle fought a year later in the same area ended in a draw, with both sides retreating and claiming victory.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-mysore-war">Fourth Anglo-Mysore War</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> iron rockets influenced British rocket development and inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congreve-rocket">Congreve rocket</a>, used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. Pullalur had also been the site of a 7th-century battle between the Badami Chalukya king Pulakesin II and the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Pollilur (1780)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Second Anglo-Mysore War (1780–1784) his army destroyed a British detachment under William Baillie at <b>Pollilur</b> in 1780, though Eyre Coote defeated him at Porto Novo in 1781.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Military</a></li></ul></section>
